BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:human-rights-watch-ai-strategy-solutions-lead-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Human Rights Watch: AI Strategy and Solutions Lead (one-y
 ear fixed term)
DESCRIPTION:Human Rights Watch is hiring an AI Strategy and Solutions Lead 
 into its Operations Department to build the foundation for responsible AI 
 adoption across the organisation. The role deliberately sits between strat
 egy and execution: assessing organisational readiness\, mapping partnershi
 ps and governance\, and also prototyping and shipping working systems. THE
  WORK\, in four strands. (1) STRATEGY AND READINESS: with the CIO and the 
 Information Security and Technology (IST) leadership team\, build an AI st
 rategy covering data foundation requirements\, infrastructure gaps and org
 anisational readiness\; assess current state and shape a roadmap balancing
  quick wins against long-term capacity\; research what peer nonprofit\, hu
 manitarian and human rights organisations are doing and find collaboration
  openings. (2) FLAGSHIP SOLUTIONS: work with departments to find high-impa
 ct use cases\, translate business needs into technical approaches\, build 
 and deploy AI/ML solutions with measurable value (time saved\, efficiency\
 , better outcomes)\, using the Microsoft AI ecosystem (Copilot Studio\, Az
 ure AI\, Power Platform) among other tools\, and oversee consultants and v
 endors to extend delivery capacity. (3) ADOPTION AND CAPACITY: balance onb
 oarding staff onto AI tools against building custom solutions where they a
 mplify the organisation's strengths\; write training material pitched at d
 ifferent work areas and skill levels covering prompting and responsible us
 e\; support working groups and communities of practice\; produce playbooks
  and guides for non-technical staff. (4) GOVERNANCE: partner with IST lead
 ership on risk assessment frameworks\, align solutions with security\, inf
 rastructure and architecture requirements\, contribute to AI policy develo
 pment and its operationalisation\, set standards for evaluating proofs of 
 concept and MVPs\, and engage business units so outcomes are co-owned\; al
 l of it balanced against data quality\, security and resource constraints 
 and HRW's mission as a human rights organisation. WHO THEY WANT: a bachelo
 r's degree or equivalent work experience in computer science\, data scienc
 e\, information systems\, mathematics\, AI/ML implementation or a related 
 technical field\; a minimum of seven years' relevant experience including 
 two to three years on AI and two to three years implementing AI/ML in prod
 uction\; experience contributing to technology strategy and assessing orga
 nisational readiness for new technology\; strong Python and ML library/fra
 mework skills\; hands-on predictive modelling\, data science and statistic
 al analysis\; experience with low-code/no-code AI platforms such as Copilo
 t Studio or Power Platform\; a track record of scoping and delivering unde
 r resource constraints\; a grasp of both generative AI and traditional ML\
 ; and the ability to explain technical concepts plainly to mixed audiences
 \, engage stakeholders and lead technology adoption as an internal partner
  to business teams. PREFERRED: nonprofit\, humanitarian or human rights ex
 perience\; data privacy\, security and ethical AI knowledge\; familiarity 
 with responsible AI frameworks and governance models\; vendor and consulta
 nt management\; and a background in politically sensitive or high-risk env
 ironments. APPLY: through the HRW job portal with a cover letter and CV\, 
 preferably as PDFs\, by 30 August 2026. Only complete applications are rev
 iewed and only shortlisted candidates are contacted\; no calls or email en
 quiries. HRW states that it does not discriminate on the basis of disabili
 ty\, age\, gender identity and expression\, national origin\, race and eth
 nicity\, religious beliefs\, sexual orientation or criminal record.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Human Rights Watch (HRW)\, Operations Department\nLocation: Mus
 t be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Amman\, Berli
 n\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, Miami\, Sao
  Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information Officer in B
 erlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsorship.\nAward
 : USD 102\,000 to 112\,000 plus employer-paid benefits if based in the US\
 ; ranges outside the US are set by local market conditions. No application
  fee.\nDuration: One year fixed-term\, full time. Application deadline 30 
 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jo
 bs/8350654002?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jobs/8350654002?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Must be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Am
 man\, Berlin\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, 
 Miami\, Sao Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information O
 fficer in Berlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsors
 hip.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dot-line-space-water-bodies-ecology-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dot Line Space Art Foundation: Water Bodies & Ecology 202
 6 (Mumbai)
DESCRIPTION:Water Bodies & Ecology 2026 is a 40-day multidisciplinary initi
 ative from the Dot Line Space Art Foundation in Mumbai\, bringing artists\
 , researchers\, collectives and communities together across research activ
 ities\, installations and exhibitions at two venues. The premise is artist
 ic engagement with water\, ecology\, climate\, memory and environmental co
 nsciousness\, and the programme is explicitly after research-based and sit
 e-responsive approaches rather than finished gallery objects\, aiming for 
 dialogue between contemporary practice and ecological concerns. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: individual artists\, artist groups\, interdisciplinary practitioners\
 , community-based initiatives\, and collectives and collaborative practice
 s\, working across all mediums and disciplines. WHAT IS OFFERED: selected 
 artists receive grants towards travel and production expenses. Be aware th
 at the published call does not give amounts\, does not mention an artist f
 ee\, and does not mention accommodation\, so confirm the terms with the fo
 undation (info@dotlinespace.co.in) before committing to travel. Applicatio
 ns go through a Google Form linked from the project page. ABOUT THE HOST: 
 Dot Line Space was co-founded in 2015 by Gourmoni Das\, Piyush Bavalia and
  Shruti Bhosle\, all graduates of Sir J.J. School of Art in Mumbai.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Dot Line Space Art Foundation\nLocation: Mumbai\, India. Presen
 ted across two venues\; the foundation operates out of the New Great Easte
 rn Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fish Art Gallery. Open to artists
  internationally.\nAward: Grants towards travel and production expenses fo
 r selected artists. The call does not publish fixed amounts and does not s
 tate an artist fee or accommodation. No application fee.\nDuration: A 40-d
 ay multidisciplinary programme in 2026 across two Mumbai venues. Deadline 
 30 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-e
 cology-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-ecology-2026/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Mumbai\, India. Presented across two venues\; the foundation opera
 tes out of the New Great Eastern Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fis
 h Art Gallery. Open to artists internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dp-curator-support-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: 2027 d/p Curator Support Open Call (Seoul)
DESCRIPTION:d/p is an independent alternative exhibition space in the Nakwo
 n Arcade\, the historic mixed-use commercial and residential complex in Jo
 ngno-gu\, Seoul built in 1968. Its Curator Support programme\, sponsored b
 y NakwonSangga\, has selected two to three independent curators every year
  since 2018\, giving each an exhibition venue\, curatorial collaboration w
 ith the d/p team\, and a modest curatorial grant to develop and realise th
 eir curatorial practice. ELIGIBILITY: individual curators or teams with at
  least one curating experience within the past three years. The applying c
 urator or team cannot participate in the exhibition as an artist\; curator
 s and artists must be clearly distinguished. SELECTION: approximately two 
 individuals or teams. WHAT IS PROVIDED: a curatorial grant of KRW 4\,000\,
 000 including tax\; use of the d/p exhibition space of approximately 187 s
 quare metres\; and a five-week exhibition slot somewhere between March and
  December 2027\, with installation and deinstallation time counted separat
 ely from the five weeks. PROCESS: first round is a document review of the 
 APPLICATION and PROPOSAL submitted in the provided format\; second round i
 s individual interviews followed by final selection. Interviews are conduc
 ted offline in principle\, but may be held online if the applicant is abro
 ad or in other exceptional circumstances. HOW TO APPLY: submit the APPLICA
 TION via the Google Form and the PROPOSAL by email to dslashp@gmail.com be
 tween 3 August and 30 September 2026. The proposal template is downloadabl
 e from the link in the @dslashp Instagram bio. File name format: '2027 d/p
  Curator Support Application_Applicant Name'. Submit as PDF\; if the propo
 sal includes video\, provide a link rather than a file. KEY DATES: submiss
 ions 3 August to 30 September 2026\; first round results announced in the 
 first week of October 2026 by individual contact\; second round interviews
  in the second week of October 2026\; final selection announced in the thi
 rd week of October 2026 by individual contact and on the @dslashp account.
  Inquiries: Minji Lee\, Curator\, dslashp@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: d/p\
 , sponsored by NakwonSangga\nLocation: d/p\, 417 Nakwon Arcade\, 428 Samil
 -daero\, Jongno-gu\, Seoul\, South Korea. Interviews are held offline in p
 rinciple\, with online interviews possible for applicants abroad or in oth
 er exceptional circumstances.\nAward: Curatorial grant of KRW 4\,000\,000 
 (including tax) per selected curator or team\, plus use of d/p's approxima
 tely 187 square metre exhibition space for a five-week exhibition and cura
 torial collaboration with the d/p team. Approximately two curators or team
 s are selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Five-week exhibition slot b
 etween March and December 2027\, with installation and deinstallation peri
 ods separate\n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1iSjEuCjay7DhL1AF1NC
 ePu-5RV3t_10WT0CNcjNNJy8/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1iSjEuCjay7DhL1AF1NCePu-5RV3t_10WT0CNcj
 NNJy8/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:d/p\, 417 Nakwon Arcade\, 428 Samil-daero\, Jongno-gu\, Seoul\, So
 uth Korea. Interviews are held offline in principle\, with online intervie
 ws possible for applicants abroad or in other exceptional circumstances.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:next-art-ai-award-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI ART AWARD: NEXT ART AI 2026
DESCRIPTION:NEXT ART AI is an open AI art competition run by the HOBAN Cult
 ural Foundation\, welcoming participants of all backgrounds regardless of 
 career\, nationality or age\, and framed as a way to reflect on the future
  of AI art through new modes of expression and artistic experimentation. T
 WO CATEGORIES: AI IMAGE ART\, for works in a single-image format at 150dpi
  or higher\, submitted as JPG\, JPEG\, PNG or BMP up to 10MB\; and AI VIDE
 O ART\, open to a wide range of video formats including animation\, motion
  graphics and live-action footage enhanced or transformed using AI\, at 10
 80p or higher\, under one minute\, 30 or 60fps recommended\, stereo 48kHz 
 audio recommended\, as mp4 (h.264) or mov. Video entries are submitted as 
 a URL via YouTube\, Vimeo\, Dropbox or OneDrive\, or an alternative platfo
 rm if those are restricted in your region\; links that are inaccessible du
 e to privacy settings may be excluded from judging. WHO CAN ENTER: individ
 uals or teams of up to three including the representative\, though you may
  not submit both as an individual and as part of a team. Team representati
 ves create the account in their own name and all members submit a consent 
 form for personal data. Applicants under 19 need a guardian's signed conse
 nt. One work may be submitted per category. RULES ON AI AND ASSETS: work m
 ust be original and created by the applicant or team\, and must be image o
 r video art created using generative AI platforms. Works that have previou
 sly won an award in another competition are not eligible. Free or paid ext
 ernal assets\, game engines\, AI frameworks\, open-source tools and editin
 g software may be used freely\, but applicants must hold licences permitti
 ng commercial use for every element\, and work infringing third-party righ
 ts is excluded. Even where external assets and editing software are used\,
  the creative process must involve AI\, whether AI-assisted direction\, tr
 ansformation\, generation or other AI-based processes\, and the choice of 
 AI tools\, the scope and method of their use\, and the prompts themselves 
 are all weighed in evaluating the creative use of AI. TIMELINE: applicatio
 ns open 1 September 2026 and close 30 September 2026 at 11:59pm KST\, prel
 iminary round early October\, final round late October\, announcement and 
 ceremony early November\, exhibition late November 2026. Organised and hos
 ted by the HOBAN Cultural Foundation\, sponsored by HOBAN Group\, with Seo
 ul Shinmun and EBN as media partners. Details and schedule are subject to 
 change at the organiser's discretion.\n\nOrganisation: HOBAN Cultural Foun
 dation\nLocation: Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibit
 ion in South Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
 \nAward: Total prize pool of KRW 21\,000\,000 across 20 winners\, paid aft
 er deduction of applicable taxes and public charges. Grand Prize KRW 5\,00
 0\,000 (selected regardless of category)\; in each of the two categories\,
  First Prize KRW 2\,000\,000\, three Second Prizes of KRW 1\,000\,000 each
 \, and five Honorable Finalist awards of KRW 500\,000 each\; plus a HOBAN 
 Group President's Award of KRW 1\,000\,000. Winners also receive plaques a
 nd certificates\, online and offline exhibition opportunities\, and a feat
 ure on the official online platform. No entry fee is mentioned.\nDuration:
  One-off competition\; exhibition late November 2026\n\nMore: https://next
 -art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://next-art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibition in Sou
 th Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:peripheral-acts-edition-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261004
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:Deadline: Peripheral Acts Edition 2: Leaning Out (UAE balcony resid
 ency)
DESCRIPTION:Peripheral Acts is an experimental\, artist-led residency progr
 amme supporting artistic production alongside dialogue\, exchange and site
 -responsive experimentation\, rooted in everyday urban life in the UAE. Th
 e second edition\, Leaning Out\, takes residential balconies as its site: 
 suspended between private life and public view\, the balcony becomes a sta
 ge for negotiations between host and guest\, inhabitant and observer\, fra
 ming questions of privacy\, voyeurism\, belonging and ownership. STRUCTURE
 : three commissioned artists take part in two phases. Phase 1 is four in-p
 erson gatherings hosted by residency programmer Shazia Salam in Dubai and 
 Abu Dhabi (1\, 7\, 14 and 21 November 2026\, roughly two hours each\; atte
 ndance at all four is expected). Phase 2 is a one-month balcony takeover p
 er artist (January\, February or March 2027)\, during which each resident 
 also hosts one (semi-)public event sharing their practice and process. The
 re is no fixed formula for what can happen on the site\; interventions are
  shaped by its conditions\, permissions and chance encounters\, carefully 
 testing the possibilities and boundaries of informal public intervention i
 n the UAE. FUNDING: AED 1\,000 commission fee plus AED 1\,500 production b
 udget per artist. ELIGIBILITY: artists currently based in the UAE with eas
 y\, ongoing access to a residential balcony (their own or one belonging to
  a friend or family member). Questions: shaziasalam.92@gmail.com. APPLY vi
 a the Google Form. DEADLINE: 4 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Peripheral A
 cts\, conceptualised and hosted by Shazia Salam and Priyanka Mehra\nLocati
 on: Dubai and Abu Dhabi\, UAE. Open to artists currently based in the UAE 
 with easy\, ongoing access to a residential balcony (their own\, or a frie
 nd's or family member's).\nAward: AED 1\,000 artist commission fee plus AE
 D 1\,500 production budget per artist. Three artists commissioned. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: Phase 1: four in-person gatherings in Dubai and Ab
 u Dhabi on 1\, 7\, 14 and 21 November 2026 (approx. 2 hours each). Phase 2
 : one-month balcony takeovers\, January\, February or March 2027 (one arti
 st per month).\n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScVzoHsSO
 bapDUhXkeK02Ya1JpxzQH3QjVRtvhfoWDM-KWeSA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScVzoHsSObapDUhXkeK02Ya1JpxzQH
 3QjVRtvhfoWDM-KWeSA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Dubai and Abu Dhabi\, UAE. Open to artists currently based in the 
 UAE with easy\, ongoing access to a residential balcony (their own\, or a 
 friend's or family member's).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mmca-international-researcher-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260918
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260919
SUMMARY:Deadline: MMCA International Researcher Residency Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:The National Museum of Modern and Contemporary Art\, Korea invi
 tes curators\, critics and independent researchers to its International Re
 searcher Residency Program at MMCA Residency Changdong in Seoul. The progr
 am supports curatorial and research-based inquiry into modern and contempo
 rary Asian art through sustained research and professional exchange with t
 he Korean art scene. Four researchers are selected across three terms in 2
 027. SUPPORT: a monthly grant of KRW 1\,200\,000 (approx. USD 850\, total 
 KRW 3\,600\,000 for the period) and a private live-in studio with access t
 o the residency's facilities. Participants cover their own round-trip airf
 are\, meals\, local transportation\, research materials and travel/medical
  insurance. ELIGIBILITY: open exclusively to curators\, critics and indepe
 ndent researchers of all nationalities except Korean citizens\, with a res
 earch focus on modern and contemporary Asian art\, at least five years of 
 professional experience and fluency in English\; previous participants of 
 the MMCA Changdong or Goyang residencies are not eligible. Applications ar
 e submitted online via mmca.go.kr only. Deadline: 18 September 2026\, 18:0
 0 KST.\n\nOrganisation: National Museum of Modern and Contemporary Art\, K
 orea (MMCA)\nLocation: Seoul\, South Korea (MMCA Residency Changdong)\nAwa
 rd: Monthly grant of KRW 1\,200\,000 (approx. USD 850)\, total KRW 3\,600\
 ,000 for the residency period\, plus a private live-in studio provided fre
 e of charge\nDuration: Approx. 10 weeks\; three terms in 2027: 1 April - 1
 0 June (1 researcher)\, 1 July - 9 September (2 researchers)\, 30 Septembe
 r - 9 December (1 researcher)\n\nMore: https://www.mmca.go.kr/eng/pr/newsD
 etail.do?bdCId=202608270010566&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.mmca.go.kr/eng/pr/newsDetail.do?bdCId=202608270010566&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Seoul\, South Korea (MMCA Residency Changdong)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:making-space-guangzhou-nl-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261003
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261004
SUMMARY:Deadline: Making Space Residency 2027 for Netherlands-Based Practit
 ioners (Guangzhou)
DESCRIPTION:The Embassy of the Kingdom of the Netherlands in China\, in par
 tnership with artist-run space Making Space in Guangzhou\, invites Netherl
 ands-based artists\, makers\, curators\, writers\, researchers and other c
 ultural practitioners to a six-week residency in spring 2027. Making Space
  (est. 2017) focuses on art and social practice with an emphasis on long-t
 erm research\, local knowledge and care-driven collaboration. The residenc
 y is process-oriented with no obligation to produce finished work\; the re
 sident takes part in at least one public exchange (talk\, workshop\, readi
 ng group\, screening\, performance or open studio). SUPPORT: EUR 2\,700 tr
 avel and artist fee subsidy (fixed per application\; duos or collectives s
 hare the amount)\, accommodation in Guangzhou\, shared studio space\, cura
 torial and research support with access to local networks\, and logistical
  support for public programming (extra funding may be considered for publi
 c programmes). The resident arranges their own China visa\; Making Space p
 rovides an invitation letter. LOOKING FOR: practitioners engaging with alt
 ernative education\, ecological justice\, migration and labour\, social pr
 actice\, intergenerational narratives\, diaspora and transdisciplinary col
 laboration\; English communication required. APPLY by email (makingspace20
 17@gmail.com) with a 200-word bio\, CV\, 2-page project proposal and portf
 olio (max 20 pages) in a single PDF\, in English. Result expected late Nov
 ember 2026. Deadline: 3 October 2026\, 23:59 CST / 17:59 CEST.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Making Space\, in partnership with the Embassy of the Kingdom of the
  Netherlands in China\nLocation: Guangzhou\, China\nAward: EUR 2\,700 trav
 el and artist fee subsidy (fixed per application\; duos or collectives sha
 re it)\, plus accommodation in Guangzhou and shared studio space\nDuration
 : Six continuous weeks between 15 March and 15 June 2027\, dates agreed wi
 th the selected resident\n\nMore: https://makingspace2017.notion.site/open
 -call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://makingspace2017.notion.site/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Guangzhou\, China
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tokyo-biennale-2027-social-dive@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tokyo Biennale 2027: Social Dive open call
DESCRIPTION:Social Dive is the Tokyo Biennale 2027 open call for art projec
 ts and artist-in-residence proposals developed through research and engage
 ment with local communities across Tokyo. Participants immerse themselves 
 in the city's neighborhoods\, from temples and historic wooden buildings t
 o business districts\, train stations and shopping streets. Selected proje
 cts are developed at 11 designated sites: Toeizan Kaneiji Temple\, Yushima
  Seido\, Otemachi/Marunouchi/Yurakucho\, Kyobashi\, Nihonbashi\, Yaesu\, K
 anda\, Ueno Station\, Tokyo Dome City\, Akihabara Electric Town\, and Baku
 rocho/Higashi-Kanda. Proposals must respond to the biennale theme From Me 
 to Us and be presented during Tokyo Biennale 2027 (1 October to 11 Decembe
 r 2027). WHAT YOU GET: JPY 100\,000 honorarium\, round-trip travel up to J
 PY 300\,000\, accommodation in Tokyo\, project development support up to J
 PY 200\,000\, venue coordination\, interpretation and translation support\
 , and an invitation letter. WHO IS ELIGIBLE: artists\, curators\, research
 ers\, collectives and practitioners in related fields\, aged 18 or older\,
  from anywhere in the world\; must be available for on-site research\, col
 laboration and implementation in Tokyo with a residency of at least one mo
 nth including the opening\; one proposal per applicant. SELECTION: two-sta
 ge evaluation on theme responsiveness\, originality\, feasibility and comm
 unity collaboration. DEADLINE: 30 September 2026\, 23:59 JST. TO APPLY: vi
 a the online form with profile photo\, personal statement (150 words)\, tw
 o key visuals\, project summary and background\, detailed description (400
  words)\, budget breakdown and schedule\, and CV or portfolio (max 5 pages
 ). Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: Tokyo Biennale\nLocation: Tokyo\, Japan
 \, at 11 designated sites. Open worldwide with no restrictions on national
 ity\, residence or base of operations\; requires on-site research and a re
 sidency of at least one month including the opening period.\nAward: JPY 10
 0\,000 honorarium (incl. tax)\, round-trip travel expenses up to JPY 300\,
 000 (economy\, one person)\, accommodation in Tokyo\, project development 
 support up to JPY 200\,000 (incl. tax)\, plus venue coordination\, interpr
 etation and translation support and an invitation letter. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Flexible residency (minimum one month including the opening
  period)\, with projects presented during Tokyo Biennale 2027\, 1 October 
 to 11 December 2027\n\nMore: https://socialdive.jp/en/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://socialdive.jp/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Tokyo\, Japan\, at 11 designated sites. Open worldwide with no res
 trictions on nationality\, residence or base of operations\; requires on-s
 ite research and a residency of at least one month including the opening p
 eriod.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stimuleringsfonds-jatiwangi-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T222014Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stimuleringsfonds: Open Call Residency Jatiwangi art Fact
 ory 2027 (Indonesia)
DESCRIPTION:The Creative Industries Fund NL (Stimuleringsfonds) is recruiti
 ng one Netherlands-based designer\, architect or spatial practitioner for 
 a two-month residency in 2027 at Jatiwangi art Factory (JaF)\, an internat
 ionally recognised artistic collective based in a former roof tile factory
  in West Java\, Indonesia. Jatiwangi's history is closely bound up with th
 e colonial history of Indonesia: the Dutch government built large roof til
 e factories in the region in the early twentieth century\, turning it into
  the most important centre of roof tile production in Southeast Asia. Now 
 that this industry has largely disappeared\, JaF develops new visions for 
 the changing rural area with clay and community empowerment at their heart
 . The residency is part of Tahun Tanah\, a recurring programme on the rela
 tionship between the people of Jatiwangi and the region's history of clay 
 and terracotta civilisation\; the 2027 theme is Elmu Ning Tanah (Learning 
 from the Earth). As resident you explore with the local community what tan
 ah (earth) means in this area\, not only as a material for the terracotta 
 industry but also as a carrier of history\, memory and culture\, and you h
 elp shape a future for the terracotta industry rooted in local knowledge\,
  traditions and the needs of the community. ELIGIBILITY: you are an indivi
 dual designer\, architect or spatial practitioner\; you are established in
  the Netherlands\, Aruba\, Bonaire\, Curacao\, Saba\, Sint Eustatius or Si
 nt Maarten (Kingdom of the Netherlands)\; you are registered in the busine
 ss register of the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK) or another business reg
 ister within the Kingdom of the Netherlands\; and you have had a professio
 nal practice in the creative industry for at least four years. NOT open to
  duos\, collectives or cooperative ventures\, nor to designers and makers 
 who receive a grant during the work period under the Talent Development Gr
 ant Scheme\, the Open Call Self-taught Designer or the Open Call Emerging 
 Design Practices ABCSSS\; accepting this open call means you cannot apply 
 to those schemes and open calls in 2027. FUNDING: EUR 4\,500 subsidy for t
 he two-month residency\; accommodation at JaF is a small private container
  house with two single beds and a bathroom/toilet. Assessment takes the fo
 rm of a tender: an external advisory committee ranks proposals on the arti
 stic quality of the work\, the quality of the proposed project and the mot
 ivation in relation to the goals of the residency\, and the highest-scorin
 g proposals are awarded. Decision within eight weeks of the closing date. 
